 \label{prop:decomp}
For odd $N \geq 1$, we have
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{FSh}_2(N) \cong \mathrm{FSh}_2(N)^{pol} \oplus \mathbb Q \widetilde{P},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{P}(X,Y)=\frac{X^{N-1}}{Y}-\frac{Y^{N-1}}{X}-\frac{X^{N-1}-Y^{N-1}}{X+Y},
\end{equation}
and $\mathrm{FSh}_2(N)^{pol}=\mathrm{FSh}_2(N) \cap V_{N-2}$ denotes the polynomial part of the Fay-shuffle space.

That $\widetilde{P}$ satisfies the Fay-shuffle equations is seen by a direct computation. On the other hand, from the definition of $\mathrm{FSh}_2(N)$, an element $P(X,Y) \in \mathrm{FSh}_2(N) \setminus \mathrm{FSh}^{pol}_2(N)$ necessarily has the form
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:decomp}
a\frac{X^{n-1}}{Y}+b\frac{Y^{n-1}}{X}+Q(X,Y),
\end{equation}
where $Q(X,Y) \in \mathbb Q[X,Y]$ is some specific polynomial, and $a,b \in \mathbb Q$ are not both equal to zero. Hence, we have 
\begin{equation}
\dim_{\mathbb Q}\mathrm{FSh}_2(N)^{pol}+1 \leq \dim_{\mathbb Q}\mathrm{FSh}_2(N) \leq \dim_{\mathbb Q}\mathrm{FSh}_2(N)^{pol}+2.
\end{equation}
But from the shuffle equation $P(X,Y)+P(Y,X)=0$, one sees that in \eqref{eqn:decomp}, it holds that $a=-b$, and thus $\dim_{\mathbb Q}\mathrm{FSh}_2(N)=\dim_{\mathbb Q}\mathrm{FSh}_2(N)^{pol}+1$.

Consider the decomposition
